1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Woldemar Graf Uxkull-Gyllenband was the son of the Earl of Woldemar Uxkull and senior writer Lucy Ahrenfeldt. The Uxkull family came from the Baltic nobility. At the age of nine years, in the autumn of 1906, [1] an
2 CONC d his brother Bernhard Woldemar were "discovered" by Uxkull-Gyllenband on the road by Ernst Morwitz, who was in close contact with the well-known poet Stefan George and looking for pupils for the George circle was. The brothers gr
2 CONC ew up in the Bavarian district of Berlin, where she taught Morwitz private. Nevertheless, their academic performance fell short of expectations, so that the high school they attended the convent school Ilfeld in 1912 in the resin.
2 CONC  As is evident from letters to his brother, he was expelled in 1915 after a "serious breach" of the Rules of the School [2] As of 1916, Woldemar prepared by Uxkull in private lessons on a high school before. Now he was in regular 
2 CONC contact with Stefan George himself. [3] In 1917 he resigned his Abitur at the Gymnasium in Berlin from spruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Uxkull was from July 1917 to January 1919 soldier in an elite unit of the Prussian Army (Guards Rifle Battalion Berlin light field), initially as a recruit, since February 1918 then in Macedonia. He began studying classical s
2 CONC tudies and ancient history, first at the University of Munich, then in Berlin and finally in Heidelberg. After he had several times been in contact before and during the war with Stefan George, Uxkull Pentecost 1919 introduced at 
2 CONC a meeting in Heidelberg jointly with Percy Gothein and Erich Boehringer he already knew from his studies in the George circle. About the circle 1919/1920 he came into contact with Ernst Kantorowicz, a close friend - Kantorowicz re
2 CONC ferred to him as "table companions and bedsteads game" [4] - and it was his most successful book devoted Emperor Frederick the second. Both lived for a time in the same house in Heidelberg, Germany. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT As a student of Alfred Domaszewskis he received his doctorate in 1922 in Heidelberg on the sources of Kimon biography of Plutarch (magna cum laude). 1923/24 is held Uxkull-Gyllenband in England, where he studied at the British Lib
2 CONC rary in London and attended lectures at Oxford. In 1925 he qualified as a professor of Ancient History at Wilhelm Weber in hall with a study of Plutarch and the Greek biography, an extension of his dissertation. After a few Visiti
2 CONC ng Professor in 1932 he adopted the reputation of the Department of Ancient History at the University of Tübingen. A call to Kiel he declined since 1934. Uxkull died in 1939 as the result of a traffic accident. He is buried in the
2 CONC  city cemetery Tübingen.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a cousin of Alexander, Berthold and Claus von Stauffenberg, who also belonged to the George circle since the 1920s. Especially Alexander, also historian who stood near him. In Tübingen Gyllenband was also a close friend of 
2 CONC Carlo Schmid. After the seizure of power by the Nazis on 30 January 1933 fell Uxkull-Gyllenband in the influence of Nazi propaganda. In a speech about the revolutionary ethos Georges at the Tübingen students on the occasion of his
2 CONC  65th birthday Georges in July 1933, he interpreted Nazism as a fulfillment of the vision of Stefan George. [6] From that time many of his old friends he turned away. Above all, the Jew as already discriminated Kantorowicz, he sen
2 CONC t a presentation copy, was horrified. His copy of the script, which he felt was insulting the Privy Germany, Kantorowicz sided with devastating asides: "Totally irresponsible" - "Miserably banal" - "in any degree of exaggeration a
2 CONC nd the poverty of offensive" [7] Uxkull was still the same. year as part of Wolfgang Frommel midnight shows in Berlin Reichssender the presentation of Sparta's downfall, in which he clearly positioned itself against the totalitari
2 CONC an Sparta and the democratic Athens. This paper can be read as a statement against the National Socialist ideology. [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Uxkull-Gyllenbands scientific focus was mainly classical Greece and the papyri as a source for the administrative and legal history of the Roman Empire. His studies on the biographies of Plutarch did not gain wide resonance, espec
2 CONC ially Felix Jacoby criticized as "remarkable simplification". [9] Very was much more influential often be acclaimed commentary on the gnomon of idios logos, which is still regarded as a standard work. [10] In this case, he spent, 
