1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Carl Friedrich Delius (Carl Delius II) links the Ronneberg to the Delius family. He is one of the key figures in the Ronneberg history. One branch of the Delius family (however, not the branch of Carl Delius II) ca
2 CONC rries the name "von Delius".
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Gut Insel (Münster) and Carl Delius' intentions
2 CONT
2 CONT Carl Delius II was a very wealthy man. His original idea was to erect a foundation for the Delius family as their home for hundreds of years. Since he lost a lot of his money by deceitful manipulations of a banker in Münster, he d
2 CONC ecided for a smaller scale solution and purchased the estate "Gut Insel" (Münster) in a series of auctions since 1823. The first auction (October 9, 1823) resulted in getting Gut Insel at a price of 3,520 Rt as hereditary tenure. 
2 CONC Upon special request of Carl Delius II, the tenure was converted into own property by additional payment of 1,200 Rt i.e. he became the owner of the core part of Gut Insel by payment of 4,720 Rt.
2 CONT
2 CONT This estate belonged to the Jesuits from 1616 until 1773. Between 1773 and 1823, Gut Insel was adminstered by the Ex-Jesuit commission and later on by the Studienfonds Münster, which supported the University of Münster and the Gym
2 CONC sasium Paulinum (grammer school) by means of the income gained from Gut Insel. By Delius' purchase in 1823, Gut Insel became the home of the Ronneberg family for 88 years. In 1911, it was sold to the city of Münster by Hermann Ron
2 CONC neberg. "Gut Vietow" in Mecklenburg (1928-1939) was meant to become a substitute of Gut Insel in the understanding of Wilhelm Ronneberg II (1883-1937). However, Gut Vietow went lost as well. An abstract of the history of Gut Insel
2 CONC , a detailed description of the land and the buildings on it in 1823, and a description of the reasons to sell it in an auction are included in the Ronneberg file of Bruno Klahn II. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The original documents about Gut Insel are preserved in the municipal archive of Münster (Stadtregistratur, Fach 55 Nr. 75 and 81) and in the Staatsarchiv of Northrhine-Westphalia in Münster (Bestand "Studienfonds Münster, alte Re
2 CONC gistratur" and Bestand "Studienfonds Münster, neuere Registratur Nr. 162" (22 files)).
2 CONT
2 CONT The influence of Delius on Ronneberg (economy, education, religion)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ronneberg family obviously benefited from the Delius connection. From the existing documents it becomes quite clear that eduation and wealth were introduced into the Ronneberg family by Carl Delius II. Without his input, the R
2 CONC onneberg family would never have gained such welfare. Without a personality such as Carl Delius II, this welfare subsequently decreased and finally ended within some 100 years. Wilhelm Ronneberg II (1883-1937) tried to restore the
2 CONC  “old glory” but finally he also failed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These facts may explain the rumor within the Ronneberg family that the family once was noble ("von Ronneberg"), that later on the nobility was given up and that Gut Insel was the home of the Ronneberg family for hundreds of years.
2 CONC  These assumptions are obviously wrong. They may be explained by the fact that one branch of the Delius family really was noble ("von Delius"), however also by the desire of one or several family members to glorify the own history
2 CONC  more than what can be justified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm Friedrich Philipp Christian Johannes (Wilhelm Ronneberg I), his father Matthias Hieronymus Ronneberg, his stepfather Carl Delius II, and the wife of the two latter, Sophia (Lange), were all protestants (Lutherans). Still, 
2 CONC Wilhelm Ronneberg I married a catholic wife. Most probably in order to avoid problems with his wife and her family or with the catholic environment in Münster, Wilhelm´s younger children (born after Delius´ death) became catholic.
2 CONC  Later on, part of the family became protestants again by marriage. The divorce of Matthias Ronneberg from Sophia Ronneberg (Lange) in 1815 was absolutely unusual and very rare at that time and might have caused tremendous problem
2 CONC s within the family, presumably especially with respect to the Ronneberg child of unknown name. The catholic baptism of Wilhelm Ronneberg I's daughter and his late sons (since 1851) may indicate a weak character and a comparably s
2 CONC trong character of his wife Gertrud Ronneberg (Holter).
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereabouts of Ronneberg's children upon Carl's marriage in 1815
2 CONT
2 CONT As a consequence of the indicated divorce, the two Ronneberg children needed new caretakers: son Wilhelm Ronneberg I obviously came directly to his mother and her new husband (Carl Delius II), the second son came to a preacher in 
2 CONC Deckbergen near Rinteln (Weser), i.e. to a region where Sophie Lange came from. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Delius family
2 CONT
2 CONT The Delius family even nowadays is rather well-known and influential. In Bielefeld they owned extensive factories that provided woven goods to the Prussian army. A number of streets is named after them. There is also a family rela
2 CONC tion to the Oetker family (pudding, bakery products) living in the same city. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two presidents of the Federal Republic of Germany are also related to the Delius family, i.e. Gustav Heinemann (1899-1976, 3 rd president 1969-1974) and Johannes Rau (1931-2006, 8 th president 1999 - 2004):
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav Heinemann's daughter Christa (*1928), sister of the more wellknown theologist and author Uta Ranke-Heinemann (*1927), married Eduard Delius. A common daughter of this marriage, Christina Delius (* October 30,1956), married 
2 CONC Johannes Rau on August 9, 1982.
2 CONT
2 CONT Assumptions about the Delius - Ronneberg connection (speculative): 
2 CONT
2 CONT Carl Friedrich Delius accepted obviously only one of the two sons of his wife, namely Wilhelm Friedrich Philipp Christian Johannes. What happened to his brother, is unknown. It seems that the brother did not benefit from Delius. T
2 CONC hat may have caused additional trouble in the family. A reasonable question therefore is: why did Delius act in this way, although he had money enough to pay for both children? One possible speculation is that Wilhelm Friederich P
2 CONC hilipp Christian Johannes was actually a biological son of Delius and the other one not. To get more evidence in this, one would have to know about the causes that resulted in the divorce. Till now, however, we do not know anythin
2 CONC g about this. The only thing known is, that the divorce became effective by a “command of the (Hessian) Elector" ("Machtspruch des Kurfürsten") which might indicate intolerable circumstances in and around that family in the unders
2 CONC tanding of that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT If Wilhelm Ronneberg I indeed is Delius´ biological son, all his descendants would belong to the Delius rather than to the Ronneberg famlily. This speculation is in reasonable agreement with the fact that the "essence" of the Ronn
2 CONC eberg family (wealth, academic education) came from the Delius side. On the other side, Matthias Hieronymus Ronneberg's descendants from his second marriage to our knowledge remained “nobodies”. Even Wilhelm Ronneberg II, who was 
2 CONC very proud of the Ronneberg family tradition, to our knowledge did not mention these decendants at all. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One can furthermore speculate that the reported deadly duell of one of the sons of Wilhelm Ronneberg, Friedrich Ronneberg, is related to some black spot on the family. However, no evidence exists at all about this speculation. 
