1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R1603628284@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=161124604&pid=162 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "This very old family of Oley township, Berks county, was founded in this township between 1720 and 1734 by David Kaufman, who came here from Germantown, Pa., where his mother resided. In 1734 he obtained a warrant for land from t
2 CONC he Penns for property located in Oley township, Philadelphia, now Berks, county. This warrant bears the date of May 24, 1734, and is for one hundred acres located in the eastern part of the township.... Later David obtained anothe
2 CONC r warrant, under date of July 1, 1740, for 54 acres, 154 perches... In addition to this property, David owned 200 acres, which he obtained prior to 1734.... All told the holdings of this Kaufman ancestor amounted to 354 acres, whi
2 CONC ch was admitted to be among the very choicest land of the township. Upon this property David erected buildings, since replaced by those more modern, but in their day they were considered models of convenience and elegance. He is b
2 CONC uried in a private burying ground upon the premises, and his resting place is marked by a limestone without any inscription. Five generations are buried in that little plot, namely David, his son, Jacob; his grandson, Jacob; his g
2 CONC reat-grandson, Jacob and his great-great-grandson, Jacob. His wife Veronica, in 1763, with her other children, released the property to her son Jacob. In the release it is stated that she was the widow of David, and therefore it i
2 CONC s evident that David died some time prior to 1763. This release is signed by the widow and her children, who were as follows: Annie Yoder, Mary Shenkle, Barbara Lasker, and John, who settled in Maidencreek township where his desce
2 CONC ndants still reside. It is evident that David had but two sons, Jacob and John." 
2 CONT ~Biographies from Historical and Biographical Annals by Morton Montgomery, JH Beers & Co Chicago 1909
2 CONT
2 CONT David Kaufman died in January, 1762, leaving a widow, Veronica Kaufman, two sons, Jacob and John, three daughters, Mary, wife of Martin Shenkle, of Oley; Barbara, wife of Nicholas Lesher, of Oley, and Anna, wife of Daniel Yoder, o
2 CONC f Union. One daughter, Susanna, having died when quite young. Veronica, the widow, with her daughters and son, John, released the homestead to the eldest son, Jacob Kaufman, Jan. 7, 1763. (We have the original release.) The son, J
2 CONC ohn, moved across the hills, as it was called in those days, and settled in the fertile valley of the Maidencreek --now known as Maidencreek and Ontelaunee townships. The son, Jacob, lived on the homestead all his life. He was mar
2 CONC ried to Hannah Hiel, daughter of Jacob Hiel, who owned a large tract of land in District township." ~by Mrs. Elizabeth K Kruger, as told in The Reading Eagle, Saturday, March 20, 1926, Page 4. "Annals of the Oley Valley A Family W
2 CONC hose Members Have Lived Close to the Soil... By Rev. P.C. Croll, D.D., of Womelsdorf
