1 NOTE Note: 
2 CONT The House of Oley, Kauffman book says the father is unknown. Frederick Balthaser's 1924 History of Berks County, says David is the son of Johann Kauffman, born 1680 in Strasburg, Germany, and Anne. And again the former book is not
2 CONC  sure where David was buried, possibly in Germantown with his mother and brother, or at the family plot in Oley Township. Balthaser says in Oley Township. David arrived in America before 1716, with his mother and brother Jacob. Ja
2 CONC cob received the Kaufman home in Germantown from mother Anne. David bought 200 acres of land in the Oley Valley from a Herbein, transaction was dated 28 November 1727. He purchased from the Penn's, or received land patents for add
2 CONC itional land, 100 acres in 1733, and another 54 acres in 1740. He also owned 175 acres in Maidencreek Township. He is considered one of the early Oley Valley pioneers. Upon his death, son Jacob received the Oley Valley homestead o
2 CONC f 300 acres and son Johannes took the 175 acres in Maidencreek Township. The original Kauffman homestead survives today at 125 acres and still owned by a family member.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Abbrev: Epitaphs - Family Graveyards in Berks, 1999
2 CONT Title: Epitaphs - Family Graveyards in Berks, 1999 
