1 NOTE TITEL: Greve, Gesandt, Geheimekonferensrσd
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT <b>Nekrolog trykt i The Gentleman's Magazine</b>
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT At the Star Hotel, Glasgow, his Excellency Count Reventlow, Ambassador from the King of Denmark to her Majesty the Queen of Great Britain. 
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Count Reventlow was sprung from one of the most illustrious families of the Scandinavian peninsula, and he carried with him in all the relations of life the spirit and deportment of a high-born gentleman. He had served his Court w
2 CONC ith distinction in the capacity of Minister Plenipotentiary in Brazil, in Portugal, at Vienna, and lastly in this country, where he found a people entirely congenial to his tastes, and a reception suited to the natural cordiality 
2 CONC of his own character. He laboured, with complete success, to improve the relations between Denmark and Great Britain. His unflinching assiduity and patriotism were incessantly engaged in providing for the defence of his country, w
2 CONC hilst his firmness and just pride in her national rights would admit of no compromise in the sovereignty of her territories. He united to a singular degree a manly impetuosity and frankness of character with a perfect mastery of t
2 CONC he details of his profession, and an exact attention to the rules and observances of diplomatic life more common in the last century than in our own.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT After many mouths spent in uninterrupted labour, he retired a few weeks ago to the estate of Sir James Matheson, in the island of Lewis, to pursue those field-sports for which he retained the characteristic attachment of youth. No
2 CONC  one who saw him starting full of spirit and energy, in the vigour of a green old age, could have imagined that he was to return no more ; and the close of his life, which took place in Glasgow, on his way back from the Hebrides, 
2 CONC was as abrupt as it is distressing, having been caused by a spasmodic affection of the heart. The Countess Reventlow and his two daughters were fortunately in attendance upon him at his decease. The body of foreign ministers in th
2 CONC is country has seldom possessed a more upright and able member ù jealous of its independence, but punctilious in its duties ; and whilst his memory will long be preserved in the affectionate relations of his family and of his priv
2 CONC ate friendships, he has deserved the highest honours his own country can pay to her statesmen, and is attended to the grave by the sincere respect of those who had known him in the land to which he was accredited by two successive
2 CONC  Sovereigns of Denmark. 
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT His body was conveyed from this country in a Danish vessel of war.
